---
const items = [
  {
    q: "What is ContentFlow App?",
    a: "It is the authenticated operator app for content planning, review, and scheduling, built to remain usable during API instability."
  },
  {
    q: "Is publishing fully automatic?",
    a: "No. ContentFlow App stays human-led. It helps prepare and organize publish-ready content, with explicit review and status transitions."
  },
  {
    q: "Why is offline behavior important?",
    a: "The app keeps supported operations visible and recoverable when services are degraded by queueing actions, preserving workspace continuity."
  },
  {
    q: "What core workflows are available?",
    a: "Feed, ideas, angles, personas, editor, scheduling/drip, feedback, integrations, projects, and diagnostics are part of the app’s daily workflow surface."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I evaluate reliability?",
    a: "Use the built-in diagnostics screens for uptime, performance, sync status, and activity; they show when the system is live versus queued."
  }
];
---

<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p>
    <h2>The main operational questions, answered directly.</h2>
    <p>
      If you want the full flow map, open
      <a href="/skill-modes">the product workflow overview</a>. If you want the
      broader list, open the <a href="/faq">full FAQ page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-list">
    {
      items.map((item) => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{item.q}</summary>
          <p>{item.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>
